You are in the part of the codebase where mistakes are expensive and slow to
surface. Read `docs/INVARIANTS.md` sections 2, 6, and 7 before writing.

## Correctness

- Filter by the tenancy key in the query. Always.
- Wrap multi-statement writes in a transaction. If two rows must agree, they must
  be written together or not at all.
- Writes that a client might retry need to be idempotent. Take an idempotency key
  or use a natural unique constraint.

## Shape of queries

- Select the columns you need. `SELECT *` is a leak waiting for the next migration.
- Every list is paginated, with a default limit and a hard cap. Prefer keyset
  pagination over `OFFSET` on anything that grows.
- No queries inside loops. If you are iterating over results and querying per
  item, that is an N+1 and it will be fine in development and terrible in
  production. Batch it or join it.
- Any column used in a `where`, `join`, or `order by` on a growing table needs an
  index. When you write such a query, say in the diff whether the index exists.

## Cost

- Anything that calls a paid API, sends mail, or runs a model gets a rate limit
  and a recorded cost. Say which limit applies.
- Long operations belong in a background job, not in a request handler. If you
  need a job queue and there is not one, stop and ask.

## Failure

- Set timeouts. Assume every external call can hang.
- Retries need backoff and a ceiling. Unbounded retry is how one slow upstream
  becomes an outage.
- Log the detail, return the generic message.
